# README Conventions

> **`@hyper-fetch/testing` is internal.** Never mention, recommend or list it here. It is not published and not part of
> the public API. Mocking for users is `request.setMock()`.

## Package README Layout (strict)

Every package README follows the root README: a banner, a links row, badges, then the sections. The banner carries the
name, headline and features, so there is no `#` title and no bold subtitle under it.

1. **Banner** - `<p align="center">` with the package banner, linked to its docs. Use the absolute URL
   `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/BetterTyped/hyper-fetch/main/.github/assets/packages/<id>.png`, never a relative
   path: npm renders the README outside the repo. The alt text names the package and repeats the headline
2. **Links row** - `<b>Quick Start</b> · Documentation · API Reference · GitHub · Discord`, centered
3. **Badges** - centered, see Badge Rules
4. `## 📖 About` - 2-3 sentences: what it does, who it's for, what problem it solves
5. `## 🎯 Key Capabilities` - persuasive feature list (see rules below)
6. `## 🚀 Quick Start` - install command + a working snippet
7. Optional reference sections a tool needs, such as `## 📋 Commands` or `## ⚙️ Configuration` (CLI only so far)
8. `## 📚 Documentation` - bullets of `**[Page](url)** - what it covers`, ending with the API Reference
9. `## 💡 Examples` - rich, package-specific code examples
10. `## 🧩 Hyper Fetch Ecosystem` - the shared logo table linking every package folder
11. `## 💖 Sponsors` - the sponsorkit image
12. `## License` - Apache-2.0 (GPL-3.0-or-later for HyperFlow)

Docs links use the live routes: guides under `/docs/guides/...` with the number prefixes dropped
(`/docs/guides/core/basics/setup`), the API reference under `/docs/api/...` (`/docs/api/core/Classes/Client`). The site
answers 200 for unknown routes, so check the page title, not the status, when verifying a link.

## Banners

Each package banner lives in `.github/assets/packages/<id>.png` and is rendered by `scripts/readme-banners/render.mjs`
from the content in `scripts/readme-banners/packages.mjs` (headline, accent color, logo, chips, code or screenshot).

- Never edit a banner by hand. Change `packages.mjs` and run `node scripts/readme-banners/render.mjs <id>`
- Re-render a banner when its package's headline, features or snippet API changes, and keep the README alt text in sync
- The code in a banner must compile against the package. Keep lines under 60 characters and chips to one row; the
  script refuses to write a banner whose text overflows
- A new package gets a `packages.mjs` entry, a banner and a cell in the Ecosystem table when it is user-facing

## Badge Rules

- Use `img.shields.io` for static and npm version badges. NEVER `custom-icon-badges.demolab.com` (unreliable)
- Downloads and bundle size use the cached badges on `api.maciejpyrc.com/portfolio/npm/` (`downloads.svg?packages=` and
  `size.svg?package=&external=`), never shields bundlephobia or bundlejs. List core and each SDK in `external`
- Order: GitHub stars (`color=333`), npm version (`color=e76f51`), downloads (`color=fbc646`), bundle size,
  `TypeScript-strict-3178C6`, the technology badge, license (`color=blue`)
- Adapter and integration packages add a technology badge with the supported range (React 18 | 19, tRPC v11, Hono 4+)

## Visual Identity

- Each package keeps its emoji for the docs and social posts:
  - ⚡ core, ⚛️ react, 🛰️ sockets, 🪄 cli, 🏛️ axios, 🔥 firebase/firebase-admin, 🎡 graphql, 🛸 orpc, 🔧 devtools plugin, ⚛️ hyper-flow, 🧹 eslint, 🐂 oxlint, 🏯 hono, 🦊 elysia
- Section headers carry emojis and capabilities use bold key terms

## Key Capabilities Rules (CRITICAL)

Key capabilities are the MOST IMPORTANT part of the README. They sell the package.

### Tone & Style
- Write from the USER's perspective. What problems do THEY stop having?
- Short, punchy, benefit-first. Not technical descriptions
- Use emoji + **bold label** + dash + benefit: `- 🔮 **Zero guesswork** - Full autocompletion for every request, response, param, and error`
- Each bullet should make someone think "I need this"
- NO generic filler like "Simple setup", "Easy to use", "Flexible". These say nothing

### Content Rules
- List 4-10 capabilities per package (more for core, fewer for small adapters)
- Lead with the most impressive/unique capability
- Focus on pain points solved: broken types? manual clients? debugging hell? offline crashes?
- Include the specific HyperFetch feature name when relevant (e.g. `useFetch`, `createRequest`)
- Keep each bullet to ONE line. If it wraps, it is too long

### What NOT to write
- ❌ "Simple setup" - everyone claims this
- ❌ "Easy to use" - means nothing
- ❌ "Flexible" - too vague
- ❌ "Built-in adapter" - not a user benefit
- ❌ Feature lists copied from other packages - each README must have UNIQUE capabilities
- ❌ Claims the source does not back. Verify every API name, option and behavior in `packages/*/src`

## About Section Rules

- 2-3 sentences maximum
- First sentence: what the package IS (identity)
- Second sentence: what it DOES for the user (benefit)
- Optional third sentence: how it relates to the ecosystem
- NO bullet lists in About — that's what Key Capabilities is for

## Quick Start Rules

- Install command first (npm install)
- Then a WORKING code snippet (3-8 lines, copy-pasteable)
- Must demonstrate the core use case of this specific package
- Include TypeScript types in the example to show type safety

## Examples Rules

- Examples come right after Documentation, before the Ecosystem table
- Each example must be a real, practical use case — not toy code
- Show things users actually do: auth, forms, pagination, error handling
- Use descriptive `### Sub-heading` for each example
- At least 2 examples per package

## Root README Special Rules

- Opens with the banner (`.github/assets/readme.png`). It carries the tagline "Fetch anything. Type everything." and
  the logos of every supported backend, so the text below must not repeat it. Keep its alt text in sync
- Under the banner: a links row, then the badge block
- "Why HyperFetch?" goes after Quick Start: a short intro, a "you usually combine" table, then grouped bullets
- "Works with" logo grid right after "Why HyperFetch?", linking each backend to its docs. Add a cell when a new
  adapter or transport ships
- Keep code examples short and readable: one idea per block, no walls of chained setup calls
- Two quick start paths: manual setup + CLI generation
- Packages table: package (repo link) with a one-line `<sub>` description, then Version, Downloads and Size badges.
  Version uses shields `npm/v`. Downloads and Size use the cached badges on `api.maciejpyrc.com/portfolio/npm/`
  (`downloads.svg?packages=` and `size.svg?package=&external=`), never shields bundlephobia or bundlejs, which time out
  or get rate limited. List core and each SDK in `external` so a size counts only the package itself
- The top downloads badge sums every published package through the same `downloads.svg` endpoint
- Sponsor section preserved

## Formatting Rules

- NEVER use em dashes (`—`) anywhere in READMEs. Use a regular dash (`-`) or rewrite the sentence instead.
